neral practice in the UK</article-title><source>Contraception</source><volume>76</volume><issue>3</issue><year>2007</year><fpage>208</fpage><lpage>214</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17707718</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><label>33</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krishnamoorthy</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Simpson</surname><given-names>C.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Townsend</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Adolescent females and hormonal contraception: a retrospective study in primary care</article-title><source>J Adolesc Health</source><volume>42</volume><issue>1</issue><year>2008</year><fpage>97</fpage><lpage>101</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18155036</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><label>34</label><mixed-citation publication-type="other">Faculty of Family Planning and Reproductive Health Care Clinical Effectiveness Unit. FFPRHC Guidance (July 2006). First prescription of combined oral contraception. 2006 (Updated January 2007), Faculty of Family Planning and Reproductive Health Care.</mixed-citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><label>35</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Halpern</surname><given-names>V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Grimes</surname><given-names>D.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lopez</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gallo</surname><given-names>M.F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Strategies to improve adherence and acceptability of hormonal methods for contraception [systematic review]</article-title><source>Cochrane Database Syst Rev</source><volume>4</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>2006</fpage></element-citation></ref></ref-list><ack><title>Acknowledgments</title><p>The authors would like to thank Dr Elizabeth Allen for her work to combine the two data sets, Dr. Andrew Copas for devising the RIPPLE weights, and Professor Anne Oakley and Professor Anne Johnson for comments on earlier drafts.</p></ack><fn-group><fn id="d30e1975"><p>The RIPPLE and SHARE (WBS U.1300.00.005) studies were funded by the UK Medical Research Council. The Health Education Board for Scotland funded the development of the SHARE program.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-group><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Girls in the RIPPLE and SHARE datasets: sample selection and characteristics</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left"/><th align="left">RIPPLE N</th><th align="left">SHARE N</th><th align="left">Combined N</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Responded at baseline</td><td>4,248</td><td>3,794</td><td>8,042</td></tr><tr><td>Responded at age 16 follow-up</td><td>3,230</td><td>3,118</td><td>6,348</td></tr><tr><td>Reported intercourse at age 16 follow-up</td><td>1,222</td><td>1,279</td><td>2,501</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded: not asked questions on pregnancy</td><td/><td>&#x02212;368</td><td/></tr><tr><td>Excluded: not asked questions on contraception at FSI</td><td/><td>&#x02212;51</td><td/></tr><tr><td>Eligible sample</td><td>1,222</td><td>860</td><td>2,082</td></tr><tr><td>Modeling sample (complete information on pregnancy)</td><td>1,109</td><td>843</td><td>1,952</td></tr></tbody></table><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Composition of modelling sample (weighted percentages)</th><th align="left">%</th><th align="left">%</th><th align="left">%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ethnic group</td><td/><td/><td/></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;White</td><td>90</td><td>98</td><td>93</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Black</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Asian</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Other</td><td>9</td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Family structure</td><td/><td/><td/></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Live with both biological parents</td><td>62</td><td>57</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Live with one or neither biological parent</td><td>38</td><td>43</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Deprivation score<xref rid="tblfn8" ref-type="table-fn">a</xref></td><td/><td/><td/></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Zero</td><td>28</td><td>18</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;One</td><td>31</td><td>27</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Two</td><td>26</td><td>31</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Three or four</td><td>15</td><td>23</td><td>18</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>FSI = first sexual intercourse.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tblfn8"><label>a</label><p>Deprivation: count of social housing, father left school at 16, mother left school at 16, neither parent in FT employment.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="tbl2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Association of contraceptive method at first intercourse with pregnancy reported at age 16 follow-up, RIPPLE and SHARE data sets</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left"/><th align="left">N (column %) in contraception group</th><th align="left">N (row %) pregnant</th><th colspan="2" align="left">Stage 1<hr/></th><th colspan="2" align="left">Stage 2 (1 + adjustment for agent FS)<hr/></th><th colspan="2" align="left">Stage 3 (1 + adjustment for deprivation, expectations and number of partners)<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="left"/><th align="left"/><th align="left"/><th align="left">OR</th><th align="left">95% CI</th><th align="left">OR</th><th align="left">95% CI</th><th align="left">OR</th><th align="left">95% CI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All girls</td><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Total</td><td>1,952&#x000a0;(100)</td><td>163&#x000a0;(10)</td><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/><td/></tr><tr><td>&#x000a0;Condom only</td><td>1,109&#x000a0;(54)</td><td>67&#x000a0;(7)</td><td>1.00</td><td/><td>1.